WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.080
SPEAKER: Hello.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.730
Today, we are going to learn
how to create a Google Compute

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.490
Engine virtual machine instance.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.620
You can create a VM using
the Google Cloud Console,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.180
the Google Cloud CLI, the
Google Cloud Platform API,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.550
or client libraries in languages
such as C#, Go, and Java.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.240
First, start with visiting
the VM Instances page

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.430
in the Compute Engine section.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.700
Here, click on the
Create Instance button.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.890
Before we proceed
further, decide

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.050
how you would like to
create the instance.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.260
That is, you could choose
to create the new instance

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.880
from scratch, using an
existing template, a machine

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.110
image, or a ready-to-go solution
from Google Cloud Platform

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.400
Marketplace.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.050
For now, we'll create an
instance from scratch.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.050
Next, specify a
name for your VM.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.270
Here are some naming
convention guidelines

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.070
that you need to follow.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.980
The name should be 1 to
63 characters in length.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.880
It must always start
with a lowercase letter.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.110
And then it can be followed
by a combination of hyphens,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.930
lowercase letters, and numbers.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.940
It should not end with a hyphen.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.530
We'll use the name
test-instance1.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.290
Now we'll add a label
to the instance.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.830
Labels help you group instances
related to a particular task

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:35.790
together.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.950
If you would like to
group this instance,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.700
add a key and a
value attached to it.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.360
We will add the label
as "usage test,"

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.230
as this is a test VM for us.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.710
Now choose a region
and zone for the VM.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.990
Note region and zone for a VM
are permanent configurations

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.500
and cannot be changed
after a VM starts.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.610
Let's create the instance
in us-central1 region

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.630
and us-central1-a zone.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.240
Beside the machine
configuration for your VM,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.730
based on your requirements
you could choose

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.380
from one of these options--

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.170
general-purpose,
compute-optimized,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.260
memory-optimized, and
GPU-based machines.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.850
Let's choose the
general-purpose E2 machine.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:32.310
Now select the machine series,
machine type, number of CPUs,

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:37.380
CPU platform, amount of
RAM, and machine GPUs.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.080
We'll choose any
2-medium type machine

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.110
with 2 vCPUs and 4 GB memory.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.600
The CPU would be
automatically selected,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.080
and we will not be adding
a GPU to this instance.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.220
Next, if you wish to capture
or record this VM's screen,

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.600
then check the option "Enable
display device" on the machine.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.760
We won't be enabling
this because we

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.700
don't have a requirement of
screen recording this VM.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.620
If you want to deploy a
container on this instance,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.340
you could walk through the
container configuration

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.260
settings for the instance.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.420
For now, we aren't deploying
a container on this instance,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.860
so we'll skip this.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:17.960
In the Boot Disk
section, you need

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.230
to select the type and
size of the instance.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:26.330
Note the default configuration
is a Debian image with 10 GB

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.770
persistent disk.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.790
You can replace this with
public images, custom images,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.630
snapshots, or existing disks.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:40.220
For this demonstration, we will
use the standard Debian GNU

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.450
Linux 11 image with a
10 GB persistent disk.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.230
If you want your applications
to invoke Google Cloud Platform

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.100
APIs, then you need to select a
service account in the Identity

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.350
and API Access section.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:56.480
Choose a service account
that has the required

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.030
level of API access.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.540
We will use a default Compute
Engine service account

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:08.030
with default type and level of
API access to ground to the VM.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.970
Now let's move to
the Firewall section.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.150
If you want to
allow HTTP or HTTPS

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:18.230
traffic for your instance, then
enable the "Allow HTTP traffic"

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.220
and "Allow HTTPS traffic"
setting checkboxes.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.890
For our instance, we prefer
to enable both these options.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.090
Finally, we are done with
specifying all configurations

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.840
for our VM instance.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.290
Review the monthly
estimate for the VM

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:36.830
with the breakdown of items
being used and sustained

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.450
use discounts applied.

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:43.530
If you are happy, then click the
Create button and start the VM.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:44.820
Congratulations.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.180
The VM has been created
and is ready to use.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.970
For more information
about creating VMs,

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.730
please refer to our
official documentation.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

